    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b396e30f-e8d5-5f7f-a8b3-f7cae507bfe3",
      ":question/problem": "Find the particular solution to $\\sin(x + 4)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-\\left(4 x + 16\\right)\\sin(x + 4)=\\cos(x + 4)$ that satisfies $y(-4 + \\frac{\\pi}{2})=\\frac{3 \\pi^{2}}{2}$. Assume that $-4<x<-4 + \\pi$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We first rearrange and separate, then integrate and apply the initial condition.\n\n$$\ny'=\\frac{\\cos(x + 4)}{\\sin(x + 4)}+4 x + 16.\n$$\n\nUse $v=\\sin(x + 4)$, so $\\mathrm{d}v=\\cos(x + 4)\\mathrm{d}x$. On the given interval, sine is positive. Thus\n\n$$\n\\int\\mathrm{d}y=\\int\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{v}+\\int \\left(4 x + 16\\right)\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n$$\ny=\\ln(\\sin(x + 4))+2 \\left(x + 4\\right)^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nAt $x=-4 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, $\\sin(x + 4)=1$ and hence\n\n$$\n\\frac{3 \\pi^{2}}{2}=\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{2}+C,\\qquad C=\\pi^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $y=2 \\left(x + 4\\right)^{2} + \\ln{\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + 4 \\right)} \\right)} + \\pi^{2}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "30f2fa4e-0685-5383-831a-867b7015491b",
